{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"1331704be419ce41","name":"card_unit_ace_of_spades","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/Homefront/units/card_unit_ace_of_spades","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"ACE OF SPADES","text":"Deployment: Deal 3 damage to an enemy. \r\nBecomes Veteran at the start of your turn.","flavorText":"The 206th Infantry Division, nicknamed “Pik-As” or \"Ace of Spades\" for its divisional insignia, was raised in East Prussia in August 1939. Initially a reserve unit for Army Group North, it joined the invasion of Poland that same year. Like most German infantry divisions, it lacked motorization and relied heavily on horses for mobility. In late 1941, the division took part in Operation Typhoon, the German offensive aimed at capturing Moscow. Positioned under XXIII Corps of the Ninth Army, it endured the brutal Soviet counterattacks during the winter. The following year, during Operation Mars, it faced intense assaults by the Soviet 39th Army in the Rzhev salient, a sector marked by high casualties and stagnant frontlines. In 1943, the division was equipped with Marder I tank destroyers and remained on the Eastern Front. In June 1944, during Operation Bagration, it was tasked with holding Vitebsk. While other German units were permitted to withdraw, the 206th was ordered to defend the city to the last man. Encircled and overwhelmed, the division was destroyed by 26 June. Its final stand marked one of the many desperate defensive efforts during the collapse of Army Group Center.","faction":"Germany","type":"infantry","rarity":"Unique","cost":6,"operationCost":1,"attack":8,"defense":8},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_ace_of_spades","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_ace_of_spades_title","SourceString":"ACE OF SPADES","LocalizedString":"ACE OF SPADES"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::infantry","attack":8,"defense":8,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":6,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_ace_of_spades_text","SourceString":"Deployment: Deal 3 damage to an enemy. \r\nBecomes Veteran at the start of your turn.","LocalizedString":"Deployment: Deal 3 damage to an enemy. \r\nBecomes Veteran at the start of your turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_ace_of_spades_history","SourceString":"The 206th Infantry Division, nicknamed “Pik-As” or \"Ace of Spades\" for its divisional insignia, was raised in East Prussia in August 1939. Initially a reserve unit for Army Group North, it joined the invasion of Poland that same year. Like most German infantry divisions, it lacked motorization and relied heavily on horses for mobility. In late 1941, the division took part in Operation Typhoon, the German offensive aimed at capturing Moscow. Positioned under XXIII Corps of the Ninth Army, it endured the brutal Soviet counterattacks during the winter. The following year, during Operation Mars, it faced intense assaults by the Soviet 39th Army in the Rzhev salient, a sector marked by high casualties and stagnant frontlines. In 1943, the division was equipped with Marder I tank destroyers and remained on the Eastern Front. In June 1944, during Operation Bagration, it was tasked with holding Vitebsk. While other German units were permitted to withdraw, the 206th was ordered to defend the city to the last man. Encircled and overwhelmed, the division was destroyed by 26 June. Its final stand marked one of the many desperate defensive efforts during the collapse of Army Group Center.","LocalizedString":"The 206th Infantry Division, nicknamed “Pik-As” or \"Ace of Spades\" for its divisional insignia, was raised in East Prussia in August 1939. Initially a reserve unit for Army Group North, it joined the invasion of Poland that same year. Like most German infantry divisions, it lacked motorization and relied heavily on horses for mobility. In late 1941, the division took part in Operation Typhoon, the German offensive aimed at capturing Moscow. Positioned under XXIII Corps of the Ninth Army, it endured the brutal Soviet counterattacks during the winter. The following year, during Operation Mars, it faced intense assaults by the Soviet 39th Army in the Rzhev salient, a sector marked by high casualties and stagnant frontlines. In 1943, the division was equipped with Marder I tank destroyers and remained on the Eastern Front. In June 1944, during Operation Bagration, it was tasked with holding Vitebsk. While other German units were permitted to withdraw, the 206th was ordered to defend the city to the last man. Encircled and overwhelmed, the division was destroyed by 26 June. Its final stand marked one of the many desperate defensive efforts during the collapse of Army Group Center."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"hasDeployment":true,"spawnCardName":"card_unit_ace_of_spades_vet","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/ACE_OF_SPADES.ACE_OF_SPADES","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":359.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_ace_of_spades_photo","SourceString":"German infantry and armor during Operation Typhoon, Eastern Front, 1941.","LocalizedString":"German infantry and armor during Operation Typhoon, Eastern Front, 1941."},"blowUpExplosionNiagara":{"ObjectName":"NiagaraSystem'NS_ExplosionInfantryL'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Niagara/NS_ExplosionInfantryL.47"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeHeavy_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeHeavy.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/Infantry/BP_AttackInfantry.BP_AttackInfantry_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_unit_ace_of_spades_C","params":[{"